The same in 3D is
{{:../elm_playground_3d/Playground3d/index.html}Playground3d}
(package {{:../elm_playground_3d/index.html}elm_playground_3d}).

{2:areas The library, area by area}

Everything a game or an application needs is written in this
repository from scratch, one idea per module, and each module's
interface explains its idea. Here they are, grouped as the README's
table groups them.

{3:playground The playground}

The APIs over the libraries below, where games meet them:

{!modules:
    Physics Audio Ai Gui Juice Multiplayer
    Ai_debug Audio_debug
}

and in 3D
{{:../elm_playground_3d/Physics3d/index.html}Physics3d},
{{:../elm_playground_3d/Audio3d/index.html}Audio3d},
{{:../elm_playground_3d/Juice3d/index.html}Juice3d},
{{:../elm_playground_3d/Multiplayer3d/index.html}Multiplayer3d}.

The layers, a camera, sprites, a tilemap, a scene:

{!modules: Camera2d Sprite Tilemap Scene2d}

and in 3D
{{:../elm_playground_3d/Camera3d/index.html}Camera3d},
{{:../elm_playground_3d/Character3d/index.html}Character3d},
{{:../elm_playground_3d/Ragdoll3d/index.html}Ragdoll3d},
{{:../elm_playground_3d/Portal3d/index.html}Portal3d}.

Other ways to program, each building a Playground [app] for you:

{!modules:
    Logo Bigbang Universe Puzzlescript Karel Povray Teletype Textmode
}

and {{:../elm_playground_3d/Logo3d/index.html}Logo3d}.

What Elm's core gives, and a few more, is
{{:../tiny_libs/index.html#core}tiny_libs' core}.

The libraries under the playground, from graphics to the terminal, know
nothing of it: they are a package of their own,
{{:../tiny_libs/index.html}tiny_libs}, whose front page lists their
modules, area by area.

{3:graphics Graphics}

The 2D and 3D rasterizers, the ray tracer, image processing, the image
and video formats: {{:../tiny_libs/index.html#graphics}tiny_libs'
graphics}.

{3:physics Physics}

In 2D and 3D: {{:../tiny_libs/index.html#physics}tiny_libs' physics}.

{3:audio Audio}

Signals, synthesis, the engine, a synthesizer's live blocks, effects, and
the files (WAV, MIDI, ABC, solfège, MOD, MP2, MP3):
{{:../tiny_libs/index.html#audio}tiny_libs' audio}.

{3:ai AI}

{{:../tiny_libs/index.html#ai}tiny_libs' game AI}.

{3:gui GUI}

{{:../tiny_libs/index.html#gui}tiny_libs' GUI}, and the
{{:../tiny_libs/index.html#juice}game juice}.

{3:networking Networking, cryptography, compression}

{{:../tiny_libs/index.html#networking}The protocols, the netcode, TLS
1.3 and the sockets}, {{:../tiny_libs/index.html#crypto}cryptography}
and {{:../tiny_libs/index.html#compression}compression}.

{3:terminal The terminal}

{{:../tiny_libs/index.html#terminal}tiny_libs' terminal}.

{3:appkits Application engines}

What a few applications share: a spreadsheet, rich text, paint and
draw, a browser's layout, editors, CAD, ... None of them draws with the
playground: a package of their own,
{{:../tiny_appkits/index.html}tiny_appkits}, whose front page lists
their modules.

The kits that draw with it are here, beside the apps they help: the
File menu, compound documents, letters in a look, a drawing's figures,
and the browsers (a page read, drawn, browsed and scripted):

{!modules:
    File_menu Component Compound Stroke_text Figure_shapes
    Browser_page Browser_draw Browser_history Browser_forms Browser_tab
    Browser_script Browser_text Browser_boxes Browser_picture Browser_url
    Browser_devtools
}

{3:gamekits Game kits}

How each genre works, what the games of a genre share: shoot 'em ups,
fighting games, platformers, mazes, puzzles, cards, adventures, racing,
sports, strategy, rhythm games, and the levels of Doom, Descent and
Comanche:

{!modules:
    Shots Path
    Hitbox Frame_data Stickman
    Tile_move Ladder Slope Crush
    Grid_move Chase Lightcycles
    Push Puzzle_undo Sokoban
    Cards Adventure
    Road Car Topdown Offroad
    Free_ball Formation Orders Rhythm
    Sectors Segments Sixdof Heightmap Isometric
}

and in 3D
{{:../elm_playground_3d/Skeleton/index.html}Skeleton} and
{{:../elm_playground_3d/Track3d/index.html}Track3d}.

{3:languages Languages}

Languages as text, parsed and run: a spreadsheet's formulas, BASIC,
s-expressions, Emacs Lisp, Scheme, Pascal, HyperTalk, Smalltalk-80,
Scratch, PostScript, JavaScript, HTML and CSS, JSON and jsonnet (the
code map's configurations), and OCaml, C and assembly read for the code
map. They know nothing of the playground either: a package of their
own, {{:../tiny_languages/index.html}tiny_languages}, whose front page
lists their modules, language by language.
